# erxes v3 core stack — copy to .env and fill in REAL values.
# Every value below is a PLACEHOLDER. Nothing here is a real secret.

# ── Image version ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Pin a release tag; do NOT use `latest` (it moves several times a day — the
# erxes CI pushes on every merge to main).
# Verified to exist on Docker Hub for core-api, gateway and ui: 3.0.61
ERXES_VERSION=3.0.61

# ── Hostnames — THREE ARE REQUIRED ──────────────────────────────────────────
# All three resolve already via the existing *.sharksmarketing.com wildcard A
# record, so no new DNS records are needed. Each must still be registered as a
# Dokploy Domain (see deploy/README.md) — DNS alone does not route.
#
# Why the UI and API cannot share one host: the gateway is a catch-all reverse
# proxy that serves core-api's routes from its own root (/initial-setup,
# /get-frontend-plugins, /core-login, /oauth/*, /upload-chunked/*, ...) as well
# as /graphql and the ws:// subscription endpoint. Those paths collide with the
# UI's own client-side routes, and the UI derives every API path from one base
# (REACT_APP_API_URL), so a single host with PathPrefix rules cannot separate
# them reliably.
ERXES_UI_DOMAIN=erxes.sharksmarketing.com
ERXES_API_DOMAIN=erxes-api.sharksmarketing.com

# Serves the Module Federation remotes (frontline_ui) — becomes PLUGIN_CDN_URL,
# which the BROWSER dereferences as
#   https://$ERXES_PLUGINS_DOMAIN/latest/frontline_ui/remoteEntry.js
# so it must be public and TLS-terminated, not an internal docker DNS name.
#
# A THIRD HOST rather than a subpath on ERXES_UI_DOMAIN: core-ui's nginx ends in
# `location / { try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html; }`, so any unmatched path
# there returns index.html with a 200 — a missing chunk would arrive as HTML
# labelled as JS ("Unexpected token '<'") instead of a clean 404. Splitting a
# subpath off that vhost means either a second Dokploy Domain with a PathPrefix
# on the same host (routing two services to one hostname, exactly the
# multiple-services conflict the compose header warns about) or rebuilding the
# core-ui image. A separate hostname costs nothing given the wildcard.
ERXES_PLUGINS_DOMAIN=erxes-plugins.sharksmarketing.com

# ── Secrets — REPLACE ALL THREE ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Signs the auth cookie/JWT. Must be identical in core-api and gateway or every
# login is rejected. Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
JWT_TOKEN_SECRET=replace-me-openssl-rand-hex-32

MONGO_USER=erxes
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 24  (avoid @ : / ? # — it goes in a Mongo URI)
MONGO_PASSWORD=replace-me-mongo-password

# Generate: openssl rand -hex 24
REDIS_PASSWORD=replace-me-redis-password

# ── Optional / not set in the minimal stack ─────────────────────────────────
# ELASTICSEARCH_URL   Set by compose to http://elasticsearch:9200. NOT optional
#                     if you use segments: erxes v3 evaluates every segment
#                     through Elasticsearch (fetchSegment.ts has no Mongo
#                     fallback), and engage targeting, inbox and form search
#                     read through it too.
#
#                     Elasticsearch alone is not sufficient. v3 removed the
#                     Mongo->ES pipeline (see saveEs.ts: "there is NO in-process
#                     Mongo->ES pipeline"), so the `monstache` service does that
#                     sync. It needs mongo to run as a replica set, which is why
#                     the mongo service starts with --replSet rs0.
#
#                     Three things must ALL hold or segments return wrong
#                     answers rather than an error:
#                       1. this URL points at a reachable cluster,
#                       2. something is syncing Mongo -> ES (our `monstache`),
#                       3. the indices are actually populated.
#                     Verify (3) after any deploy that touches this:
#                       curl -s 'http://elasticsearch:9200/_cat/indices?v' | grep erxes__
#                     erxes__companies / erxes__customers must show a non-zero
#                     docs.count. Empty indices are indistinguishable from
#                     "this segment matched nothing".
#
# ELK_SYNCER          Unset here, which means TRUE — getEnv defaults it to
#                     'true' and only the exact string 'false' disables it.
#                     Read in two places, and they behave differently:
#
#                       saveEs.ts  — gates saveEsDoc/deleteEsDoc, described
#                         upstream as mirroring a point-in-time revert back into
#                         ES. Both are exported but have NO callers anywhere in
#                         the tree, so this branch is currently dead either way.
#
#                       broadcast/utils/engage.ts — picks the code path for
#                         campaign audience checks. TRUE queries Elasticsearch;
#                         FALSE runs an equivalent Mongo query. So broadcast has
#                         a real fallback that segments do not.
#
#                     Set it to 'false' ONLY if you deliberately run without
#                     Elasticsearch. That keeps campaign targeting working off
#                     Mongo, but segments still cannot be evaluated — they have
#                     no non-ES path at all.
# ENABLED_PLUGINS     Defaults to `frontline` in compose — never "" (see the
#                     compose header: "" yields a phantom nameless plugin and
#                     the gateway never serves /graphql). Adding a plugin means
#                     adding a service named EXACTLY plugin-<name>-api AND
#                     serving its <name>_ui build under PLUGIN_CDN_URL, else
#                     the browser 404s on its remoteEntry.js.
# PLUGIN_CDN_URL      Derived from ERXES_PLUGINS_DOMAIN above; only override it
#                     directly if you host the remotes somewhere else entirely.
# S3/AWS_*            Needed for file upload; unset = uploads fail.
# SENTRY_DSN / REACT_APP_SENTRY_DSN   Error tracking, optional.
